WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.698 line:5%
1902. A leaky shed at a Paris science school.

2
00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:09.480
For nearly four years, Marie Curie has
been processing tons of ore here, by hand.

3
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:11.738
One night, she turns down the lamp.

4
00:00:15.917 --> 00:00:17.318
Her radium is glowing.

5
00:00:21.417 --> 00:00:27.828 position:68% size:60%
It began in 1896, when Henri Becquerel
found that uranium gives off invisible rays.

6
00:00:28.042 --> 00:00:30.582
Curie measured them with an electrometer,

7
00:00:30.582 --> 00:00:33.590
which detects the tiny current
the rays create in air.

8
00:00:33.792 --> 00:00:37.300
The strength depended only on
how much uranium was present.

9
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.245
Wet or dry, powder or solid,
it didn't matter.

10
00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:44.296
So the rays came from
the atoms themselves.

11
00:00:44.296 --> 00:00:46.078
She named it radioactivity.

12
00:00:46.292 --> 00:00:51.690
But pitchblende, a uranium ore, was more
radioactive than its uranium could explain.

13
00:00:51.875 --> 00:00:53.844
Something else was inside.

14
00:00:53.844 --> 00:00:57.403
In 1898, the Curies announced
two new elements:

15
00:00:57.403 --> 00:01:03.082
polonium, for Marie's homeland, Poland,
and radium, from the Latin for ray.

16
00:01:03.292 --> 00:01:06.573
Proving radium was real meant
extracting it from the ore.

17
00:01:06.573 --> 00:01:08.588
That's the work we saw in the shed.

18
00:01:08.792 --> 00:01:10.828
Today we know why it happens:

19
00:01:10.828 --> 00:01:16.585 position:72% size:56%
atoms like radium have unstable nuclei,
and as they decay, they release radiation.

20
00:01:16.792 --> 00:01:20.244
Radioactivity comes from inside the atom.

21
00:01:20.244 --> 00:01:22.709
Curie proved it by measuring carefully,

22
00:01:22.709 --> 00:01:25.687 line:5%
and it earned her Nobel Prizes
in two different sciences.
